{
  "family": "ownership",
  "description": "The directed-graph reading of ownership, pinned as explicit known answers (Issue# 650 - tap; the concept and its eventual escape hatch are Issue# 649 - tap). Every consumer of ownership — the cascade, candidate derivation, the falsifier's owner comparison — reads the one CONTAINMENT_EDGES declaration, so 'belonging' is a directed edge from a parent. These scenarios name where that reading is a choice rather than a fact: a child with a second live parent outside the subtree retires with the first parent that cascades (the cascade warns); a plain delete of one parent leaves the child live and ends only that edge; a reference edge from outside never counts as ownership; every corpus type is keyless, so every parent here is the 'grid holds no source identity for the parent' case the falsifier handles by not comparing owners. When a real relationship stops fitting this reading, the case goes here first, and #649 leaves Backlog.",
  "scenarios": [
    {
      "name": "shared child: cascade from one parent retires it although the other parent is live (the directed-graph reading)",
      "tags": [
        "ownership",
        "shared-parent"
      ],
      "covers": [
        "req-grid-service-delete-cascade-1"
      ],
      "graph": {
        "containment": {
          "grid_fixtures__node": [
            "PG_NESTS__grid_fixtures"
          ],
          "grid_fixtures__hub": [
            "PG_NESTS__grid_fixtures"
          ],
          "grid_fixtures__leaf": [
            "PG_NESTS__grid_fixtures"
          ],
          "grid_fixtures__cycle_node": [
            "PG_NESTS__grid_fixtures",
            "PG_LOOPS__grid_fixtures"
          ]
        },
        "nodes": [
          {
            "ref": "P",
            "type": "grid_fixtures__node"
          },
          {
            "ref": "R",
            "type": "grid_fixtures__node"
          },
          {
            "ref": "D",
            "type": "grid_fixtures__node"
          }
        ],
        "edges": [
          {
            "ref": "e_P_D",
            "from": "P",
            "to": "D",
            "type": "PG_NESTS__grid_fixtures"
          },
          {
            "ref": "e_R_D",
            "from": "R",
            "to": "D",
            "type": "PG_NESTS__grid_fixtures"
          }
        ]
      },
      "operation": {
        "verb": "delete_node",
        "target": "R",
        "cascade": "contained"
      },
      "expected": {
        "outcome": "success",
        "error_code": null,
        "retired_nodes": [
          "R",
          "D"
        ],
        "retired_edges": [
          "e_R_D",
          "e_P_D"
        ],
        "note": "The corner case: D belongs to P and to R; R's cascade retires D and ends P's edge into it because it is incident. Ruled the same as loops' 'second containing parent' case; the cascade logs a warning naming P (Issue# 650 - tap). If shared ownership should keep D alive, that is the per-type ownership logic #649 holds."
      }
    },
